rspsdr (1.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Five colour schemes for the window, chosen from a Theme list and
    remembered: Night (the default -- the near-black blue with a cyan trace the
    window has always had), Amber (a valve set: black dial, amber markings),
    Phosphor (green cathode-ray glow), Slate (neutral grey with one soft blue
    accent) and Daylight, which is light, for a bright room or a projector.
    One table in rspsdr/theme.py sets the palette, the controls, the plots'
    backgrounds and pens, the grid and the passband wash, plus the labels that
    used to carry a hex value of their own, so a scheme is a name rather than a
    dozen colours scattered through two files.  --theme NAME opens in one
    without touching what is remembered; --check names them and says which the
    window would open in.
  * The schemes are checked for readability rather than left to the eye: the
    unit suite computes WCAG contrast ratios and requires ordinary text at
    7:1 against the window, the spectrum trace at 4.5:1 against the plot it is
    drawn on, the marker, the peak trace, the passband wash and the grid above
    floors of their own -- and the grid quieter than the trace in every one of
    them.  A scheme that cannot be read now fails the run.
  * The signal meter has moved to the top right of the display area, in the row
    above the picture, with the same reading in figures printed beside it.  It
    was in the gain row, among a dozen boxes, where the one instrument in the
    window meant to be read at a glance was the hardest thing to find; it is
    now where a transceiver puts it.  Its own setup -- the S9 calibration, the
    peak marker and the face -- moved down with the rest of the settings.
  * Everything below the picture -- theme, meter setup, gains, FFT size,
    waterfall levels, colour, detail and zoom -- is now one pane that folds
    away (the button at its top left, or Ctrl+H), and folding hands the rows'
    height to the picture: at 1360x739 the two plots go from 275 px together to
    369, so the waterfall gains 94 px of screen it did not have.  The state is
    remembered, so a window left folded opens folded.
  * The window's shortest height now follows what it is showing.  With the
    pane folded it can be 780x560 as before; with the rows on show it refuses
    to be shorter than they are (659 px at the narrowest width), because Qt
    would otherwise squeeze the picture to nothing and then cut the rows off at
    the bottom edge -- measured before the fix: the +/-/Fit zoom buttons 18 px
    below the bottom of the window, visible according to Qt and reachable
    according to nobody.
  * tests/test_units.py: every scheme complete, every colour a #rrggbb that
    parses, the readability ratios above, and the style sheet built from each
    one balanced, fully filled in, naming every colour the controls use, and
    never styling a bare QWidget (such a rule reaches the plots and paints over
    their own background).
  * tests/test_prefs.py: the colour scheme and the folded state are written to
    settings.json and come back -- with the scheme on the plots rather than
    only in the combo box that names it, and a folded window opening folded and
    small again.  Twenty-five controls now round-trip.
  * tests/gui_smoke.py: the meter's placement is checked as geometry (top
    right, above the spectrum and so above the waterfall); the rows below the
    picture are one pane whose folding really does give its height to the
    picture (the pane shrinks, the plots grow by what it gave up, the top of
    the picture does not move, the waterfall's history gains rows), the fold
    button stays on screen when everything else is put away, Ctrl+H does the
    same, and the window will not shrink past the rows it is showing while the
    folded window still reaches 780x560.  Every scheme is walked and required
    to reach the plots, the stroked labels and the application's style sheet.
  * The zoom ladder runs to 256x instead of 16x -- nine doubling steps, and at
    2 MSps a 7.8 kHz window (244 Hz at the lowest sample rate) where the old
    top of the ladder showed 125 kHz.  It stops where the arithmetic stops: the
    window is the receiver span divided by the zoom, the FFT divides that span
    into bins, so the bins across the window are the FFT size divided by the
    zoom, and at the smallest transform on offer (1024 points) the deepest step
    still has four of them.  The width readout follows the ladder down -- Hz,
    kHz or MHz -- because "0 kHz" would have called a 244 Hz window nothing,
    and the +/- buttons say when the ladder has ended instead of looking broken.
  * tests/gui_smoke.py: the ladder is walked end to end against the smallest
    FFT, requiring each step to halve the window, the bins across it to be the
    FFT size over the zoom, and the deepest step to still have bins to draw.
  * tests/test_units.py: the ladder and the FFT list are checked against each
    other -- doubling steps from 1x, the deepest step four bins wide at the
    smallest transform on offer, and the window still a real width at the
    lowest sample rate, so the readout cannot come out as "0 Hz".
  * README: the schemes, what folding the controls away buys, why the
    window's floor depends on what it is showing, and where the zoom ladder
    stops.

