rspsdr (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * An analogue signal meter, drawn to look like the S-meter of an Icom HF
    transceiver: a needle sweeping a shallow arc over a printed dial, S1 to S9
    and then +20/+40/+60 dB, in either the dark face of the later radios or the
    cream one of the older analogue sets.  It is painted rather than assembled
    from widgets, so the needle pivots below the face the way a real one does
    and the top of the scale is compressed the way a printed dial is.
  * The scale is the IARU recommendation -- S9 is -73 dBm (50 uV into 50 ohms
    at the antenna socket), 6 dB per S unit below it, the standard's 10 dB steps
    above -- and the needle has the recommended quasi-peak ballistics: 10 ms
    attack, 500 ms decay, with a peak marker holding the highest level for half
    a second so a signal that comes and goes between repaints is not missed.
    The exact figure is printed along the foot of the face as well, because a
    needle can only be read to its own marks.
  * It measures the level *inside the filter being listened to*, taken before
    the audio AGC, the noise reduction, the squelch and the volume: it does not
    follow the volume control, it does not care how hard the noise reduction is
    working, and it still shows a signal you cannot hear because the squelch is
    shut (measured: the same level to the last bit with the volume at 2 % and at
    full, and with the squelch open and closed).  A carrier 18 kHz away moves
    the status bar's level by 15.7 dB and the needle by 0.00 dB.  Stopping the
    receiver drops the needle back to its rest instead of leaving it standing at
    the last signal.
  * Its absolute accuracy is one number the user supplies, "S9 at": the RSP1
    reports a level in dBFS from after the gain stages and has no absolute
    calibration of its own, so the dBFS reading that is to count as S9 is asked
    for and the whole scale follows from the standard.  The default comes from
    measurements on a real RSP1 (2 MSps, 6 kHz AM, the default IF gain of
    40 dB): a whole band of HF noise reads S6.7 on 31 m, S6.2 on 41 m and S5.3
    on 19 m, and a 10 dB change of gain moves the needle by exactly 10 dB
    (measured at 9.805 MHz), so one calibration holds wherever the gain is left.
    The bottom of the scale is the calibration's business rather than the
    hardware's: S1 is -121 dBm, under the RSP1's own noise floor even at its
    lowest gain, where the noise still reads S3.8.  --selftest now prints the
    passband level and what the meter makes of it, which is how the calibration
    is checked against a station of known strength.  The calibration, the peak
    marker and the face are remembered with everything else.
  * tests/test_units.py: the scale (S9 at -73 dBm, 6 dB per unit, 10 dB steps
    above, the words an operator would say), the dial (evenly spaced marks,
    every level round-tripping through the needle to within half a mark, the arc
    monotonic end to end) and the ballistics (63 % of a step in one attack time,
    the decay no faster than its constant, the half-second hold, the peak never
    below the needle).
  * tests/test_chain.py: a new section on the level the meter reads -- 6.02 dB
    for every halving of the carrier, one S unit per halving either side of S9,
    the same level with the noise reduction on, the audio AGC on, the volume at
    2 % and the squelch shut, and a carrier outside the passband moving the
    baseband level but not the meter.
  * tests/test_prefs.py: the meter's calibration, peak marker and face come back
    after a restart, and the widget with them -- the level it was calibrated at
    reads S9 again, where the default calibration would have read S6.4.
  * tests/gui_smoke.py: the needle is drawn where the dial geometry puts it,
    checked in pixels on both sides of centre and on both faces; the peak marker
    still holds a signal the needle has left behind; stopping the receiver lets
    the needle fall back to its rest and the face say so.
  * Fixed: the GUI smoke test wrote the user's own settings file.  It drives
    every control in the window, and the window remembers them, so running the
    test reconfigured the person running it.  It now keeps its settings in a
    config directory of its own, as the other suites already did.
  * README: the meter, the IARU scale it uses, what it measures and what it
    therefore ignores, and how to calibrate it against a signal of known
    strength.

 -- RockIslander <174265838+RockIslander@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:40:00 -0230

rspsdr (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The window now remembers everything between runs, so a listening setup
    survives closing the program.  Every control -- frequency, mode and filter,
    CW pitch, IF and RF gain, tuner bandwidth, RF AGC, volume, noise reduction
    and its strength, audio AGC, squelch, FFT size, tuning step, zoom, the
    waterfall's colour map, levels, detail and tracking, the FT8 band and
    whether it decodes every slot -- is written to ~/.config/rspsdr/settings.json
    as it changes (1.5 s after it stops changing) and read back at the next
    start, together with the window's own size, place and maximised state and
    the divider between the spectrum and the waterfall.  The file is written
    atomically, so an interrupted write leaves the previous settings rather than
    a truncated file that reads as "nothing remembered".
  * Every value is read back through one table that says what kind of thing it
    is and what range or set of values is usable; anything that fails is ignored
    and the built-in default kept.  A hand-edited or half-written file therefore
    costs one setting, not a window that will not open: not JSON at all, a list
    where an object belongs, an empty file, a volume of 99, a mode name the
    demodulator would raise on -- each is survivable, and the values around it
    still apply.  Keys a newer version wrote are kept rather than dropped.
  * The command line overrides the file, but only where it is typed, which
    needed a second read of argv with every default replaced by a marker: not
    typing --agc and typing it were previously the same value, so a remembered
    "RF AGC on" would have been undone by silence.  Asking for a mode without
    asking for a filter gets the width that suits the mode being asked for
    rather than the one belonging to the mode being left behind.  A headless run
    (--selftest, --wav, --ft8, --save-memory) deliberately ignores the file
    altogether: a script has to get the same result every time, whatever the
    window last saved.  --reset-settings empties the file before the window
    opens, so a crash on the way up cannot leave the values it was asked to be
    rid of, and --screenshot is given no file at all -- a scripted look at the
    window must neither be configured by the user's settings nor clobber them.
  * FT8 borrows three settings instead of changing them.  Entering the mode
    still switches off noise reduction, the audio AGC and the squelch, but each
    is now remembered and put back on the way out -- and closing the window
    while in FT8 writes down the values the user had, not the ones the decoder
    wanted, so a session spent decoding no longer costs the listening settings
    for the modes you go back to.  A setting the user changed *while* in the
    mode is theirs and stays: only a value still sitting exactly where FT8 put
    it is restored.
  * Fixed: the squelch box showed -140 dB whatever the receiver was actually
    doing -- it was initialised to a constant rather than to the setting -- and
    the RF gain and tuner bandwidth boxes always showed their first entry (0 and
    *Auto*) however they were set, so a window opened from the command line or
    from a memory reported one thing and received another.
  * The window's own shape is remembered with the rest: the divider between the
    spectrum and the waterfall is written down where it was dragged, and comes
    back there, alongside the size, the place and whether it was maximised --
    which is now actually restored rather than only written down, and only onto
    a screen that still exists.  The place is saved as the frame's corner and
    the size as the client's, because that is what move() and resize() take:
    saving the client position, which sits a title bar below the frame, walked
    the window down the screen by the height of its own decoration on every
    restart.
  * tests/test_prefs.py: 77 checks with neither a radio nor a display.  The
    window is opened offscreen with the device left unopened, twenty controls
    are changed as a listener would, and the window is closed and opened again
    with every one of them required back -- size, place and borrowed FT8
    settings included.  It also covers what a restart cannot show: every value
    in the table accepted and refused, four kinds of unusable file, one bad
    value not costing the others, no half-written temporary file left behind,
    the command line winning only where it is used, a headless run ignoring the
    file, --reset-settings, --screenshot, and a remembered position off the edge
    of every screen being ignored rather than applied.
  * tests/run_all.sh runs the settings suite every time (it needs no radio).
  * README: what is remembered, where it lives, what is deliberately not (mute,
    the band and preset pickers), and how to get back to the defaults.

 -- RockIslander <174265838+RockIslander@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:20:00 -0230

rspsdr (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * FT8: a decoder built into the receiver.  The mode row has an FT8 button
    that turns the receiver into an FT8 decoder: USB with a 3 kHz filter, and a
    strip along the foot of the window with the standard WSJT-X dial frequency
    for every band from 160 m to 2 m, a slot clock, and a list of the messages
    decoded -- one line per message, showing the UTC slot, the SNR, the time
    offset, the audio frequency and the text, newest at the bottom.  Clicking a
    line tunes to the frequency that message was heard on.  Choosing a band
    tunes the receiver to its dial, and because FT8 signals sit above the dial,
    a signal at 1500 Hz on 20 m is 14.0755 MHz.  Entering the mode also
    switches off the three settings that are good for listening and bad for
    decoding -- noise reduction, the audio AGC and the squelch -- since jt9
    estimates its own noise floor and wants the passband as it arrived.  A
    memory channel saved in FT8 mode comes back in it.
  * FT8 is decoded, not written.  WSJT-X's jt9 is the decoder the FT8
    community runs; the new rspsdr/ft8.py is everything around it that is
    specific to this program: the band presets, cutting the receiver's 48 kHz
    audio down to the 12 kHz jt9 reads, aligning that cut to the UTC quarter-
    minute, and turning jt9's output into something a window can show and click
    on.  A finished slot goes to a worker thread, so the decode of one slot
    happens while the next is already being received -- the only way to keep up
    with a band that transmits every fifteen seconds.  Nothing is decoded
    without the decoder: with WSJT-X absent the mode still works as a receiver
    and says what is missing instead of failing every slot, and the failure is
    reported once rather than four times a minute.
  * FT8 also runs headless: `--ft8` decodes from the command line and prints
    each message as it arrives, `--mode ft8` opens the window in the mode with
    the decoder already armed, and `--check` names the decoder (or how to
    install one) alongside the band presets.
  * Fixed: FT8 decoded the first slot and then stopped for ever, with perfectly
    good audio arriving.  A slot is 180000 samples, and the collector worked
    out where the next slot began by asking the audio clock again for the next
    multiple of fifteen seconds; a slot's worth of additions of 1/12000 s
    drifts by a few microseconds, and a few microseconds past a boundary made
    that question return the boundary after the one it meant -- so it skipped
    fifteen seconds of audio, then the next fifteen, indefinitely, while the
    strip went on saying it was listening to every slot.  The grid is now
    advanced by whole slots from the slot just finished, with the clock
    consulted only to find the first one, and a slot that begins a hair late is
    stamped with the boundary it is inside.  Measured over ten minutes of
    synthetic audio: 40 boundaries, 40 slots captured, against one before.
  * Fixed: the mode in use was not the mode lit up.  The window opened with no
    mode button pressed at all, and the button the receiver was actually
    following was only ticked once the user clicked something.
  * The DSP tap the decoder listens to is taken *before* the volume and mute,
    so what FT8 hears does not depend on either.
  * tests/test_ft8.py: 119 checks with neither a radio nor a sound card.  It
    drives synthetic audio through the real spotter with a clock the test
    moves -- slot arithmetic, the drop of everything before a boundary, the
    WAV handed to jt9 (mono, 16-bit, 12 kHz, exactly one slot, one gain for the
    whole slot), the decimation (level and frequency survive, everything above
    the new Nyquist is filtered rather than folded, and the output does not
    depend on the block sizes it is fed), ten minutes of audio giving forty
    slots on forty boundaries, a decoder that falls behind dropping slots
    rather than queueing them, and a failure in the decoder path being reported
    and survived instead of taking the receiver's DSP thread down with it.  It
    then runs the whole chain with WSJT-X at both ends -- ft8sim encodes a
    known message, the spotter cuts it out of 48 kHz audio arriving in blocks,
    and the real jt9 decodes it, with the message, the frequency, the slot and
    the SNR checked -- and drives the window: presets, the band list, the
    decode table, tuning on a click, the cap on the list, the receiver never
    being handed a mode called "ft8", and --mode ft8 opening with the strip up.
  * tests/run_all.sh runs the FT8 suite every time (it needs no radio) and
    adds a live check of the headless decoder path to the hardware group.

 -- RockIslander <174265838+RockIslander@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:15:12 -0230

rspsdr (1.0.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed: the waterfall was drawn at the wrong horizontal scale, which made
    tuning feel inaccurate.  An ImageItem scales a rectangle by the width of
    the image it already holds, and the rectangle was applied before the new
    row was handed over -- so it was built from the previous column count.  The
    first frame was drawn 37.6 MHz wide inside a 2 MHz window, and the width
    ratio came out again after any resize or detail change; on a detail change
    a 6400-column picture was placed 6463 px wide in a 1325 px plot.  The
    axis, the clicks and the drags were all correct throughout, so a signal
    appeared at a frequency nothing like the one the window reported, and
    tuning to the carrier on screen landed somewhere else.  The row is handed
    over first, then placed.
  * Fixed: a drag moved the window out from under the pointer.  The automatic
    re-centre ran during the gesture, so a drag across 90% of the half-span
    jumped half a window and then tuned in steps of it -- a 10 px move retuned
    by 900 kHz -- and the frequency under the cursor changed while the user was
    still holding it.  Re-centring is held off until the button comes up, when
    it happens once, and the pointer-to-frequency mapping is frozen for the
    duration of the gesture, so nothing that repaints meanwhile (a re-centre
    commanded just before the press, a resize, the window following the dial)
    can shift it either.
  * Clicks and drags read their frequency from the transform the frequency
    scale is drawn with, sampled at the edges of the plotting area, instead of
    being worked back out of the axis range and the widget's rectangle.  The
    two disagreed by about a pixel -- half a bin at a 4096-point FFT -- so
    clicking a carrier in the waterfall could land 500 Hz from the frequency
    the labels showed, and a right-click in the spectrum re-centred 590 Hz off.
  * The receiver now recovers when the SDRplay device is busy at startup
    instead of giving up until the program is restarted.  The front end serves
    one client at a time, so while another program holds the RSP1 an open
    fails at once with "no available RSP devices found" and the device cannot
    even be enumerated -- which is why the window used to say "no SDR found",
    then "error: no available RSP devices found", and never look again.  It
    now says the device is busy, keeps asking (after 1 s, then every 5 s) and
    starts streaming by itself the moment the other program closes, clearing
    the message and putting the device name back in the header.  It stops
    after five minutes without the device and says so; Start begins again.
    Failures that waiting cannot fix (missing bindings, an unknown driver) are
    reported at once rather than hidden behind a retry.
  * The receiver also recovers when the stream dies underneath it, rather than
    freezing.  Unplugging the radio, restarting the SDRplay service or a driver
    wedged after a resume all arrive the same way: readStream stops returning
    samples and raises nothing, and reading it again is what happened for ever
    -- a frozen waterfall and a Stop button that stopped nothing.  Three
    seconds of silence now ends the stream properly: both worker threads stop,
    the window releases the device, says what happened, and looks for the radio
    again with the retry it already uses at startup -- so plugging it back in
    is enough and the program does not have to be restarted.  The check is
    skipped while the radio is being stopped on purpose, so the rate change
    that closes and reopens the stream cannot report a loss it caused itself,
    and a stream that is delivering samples is never mistaken for a dead one.
  * Radio.open() is now atomic: a failure part-way through unmakes the device
    it had already made, so `opened` cannot lie and a retry starts from a clean
    front end rather than from a half-claimed one.
  * New test suite, tests/test_startup.py, covering all of the above with the
    SoapySDR module and the audio backend stood in for, so no radio is needed
    and a busy device can be produced to order.  It also covers a stream that
    goes quiet mid-session, including the false positive that matters (a
    delivering stream must not be declared dead) and the intentional stop.  The
    GUI suite gained checks
    that the drawn rectangle covers exactly the window the axis shows (measured
    from the axis range and the plot rectangle, not from the plot's own
    transform) and that a drag tracks the pointer without moving the window;
    both fail against the previous code.

