rspsdr (1.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Waterfall resolution reworked along SDR++'s lines.  The default detail
    setting, "Screen", builds each line from as many columns as the plot has
    pixels (SDR++'s dataWidth), so the picture is 1:1 with the display and
    nothing is stretched; the history is one line per pixel of plot height;
    and bins are reduced to columns by maximum (SDR++'s doZoom), so a carrier
    that falls between two columns keeps its strength instead of being
    averaged into the noise.  The FFT list now reaches 32768 and 65536 points.
  * Fixed: the waterfall drew every frequency to the left of where the axis
    said it was.  Pooling grouped the first k*columns bins and dropped the
    remainder, then stretched what was left over the whole span -- 97% of the
    span drawn into 100% of it, 20 px (30 kHz at 2 MSps) out in the middle of
    the window and 57 kHz at its edge.  Bins are shared evenly among the
    columns now, and the spectrum trace reaches the right-hand edge.
  * Performance on a CPU-rendered display: a repaint of the window cost 92.5
    ms against a 45 ms interval (11.7 fps); it costs 11.1 ms (22.7 fps) now.
    The spectrum transform moved to scipy.fft (5.2 -> 2.1 ms for sixteen
    16384-point transforms of complex64, and it keeps single precision),
    antialiasing was turned off for the plots (81 -> 21 ms a frame; the
    waterfall image asks for smooth scaling on its own), the grid draws two
    levels of ticks instead of three (20.5 -> 14.4 ms), each plot is drawn
    with one point per pixel, and the waterfall keeps its history as pixels as
    well as dB rows so a frame colourises one row rather than the whole
    picture (21 ms -> 0.1 ms), with a level or colour change rebuilding the
    picture from the dB rows.
  * Tests: the GUI suite checks that Screen equals the plot's pixel width and
    follows a resize, that a lone carrier survives pooling, that drawn pixels
    are the colour map's entry for the level behind them, that a cleared
    history is black under a reversed palette, that the drawn carrier lands on
    the pixel the axis names, and that a full repaint fits the repaint
    interval; the unit suite pins the transform against a plain numpy
    reference.

